<p>The <strong>Buy</strong> area handles everything from ordering to paying: suppliers, purchase orders, deliveries, supplier invoices
  and what you owe. Stock clerks order and receive; accountants handle invoices and payments.</p>

<h2>Suppliers</h2>
<p>Add each supplier in <strong>Buy › Suppliers</strong> with their contacts, payment terms and, if they invoice in dollars, their
  currency. On the supplier's page you can list the products they supply, with their code, price, lead time and minimum order, and mark
  them as the preferred supplier.</p>
<p>Moving from another system? The owner can bring every supplier in at once from a spreadsheet, with what you owe each one today:
  on <strong>Suppliers</strong>, open the <strong>⚙</strong> menu and choose <strong>Import suppliers</strong>, fill in the
  <a href="/supplier-import-template.csv" class="link">template</a> (only <code>name</code> is needed) and upload it as CSV. HardPoint checks
  the whole file first, and imports nothing if any line has a problem. A <code>balance</code> goes on as an invoice called
  <strong>OPENING BALANCE</strong>, dated <code>balance_date</code> (today if left out) and due after the supplier's payment terms; your
  payments settle it like any other invoice. It isn't a purchase, so it stays out of the VAT report. A balance is only set for a supplier
  with no orders, invoices or payments yet, so importing the same file twice can't double what you owe.</p>

<h2>Ordering</h2>
<ol>
  <li>In <strong>Buy › Purchase orders</strong>, press <strong>New</strong>, choose the supplier and branch, and add the products. To order
    by the box or bag, type one of the product's pack sizes under <strong>Pack</strong>; the quantity and cost are then per pack, and
    stock still counts the pieces.</li>
  <li>Press <strong>Email to …</strong> to send it with a PDF attached, or <strong>Mark sent (phoned in)</strong> if you ordered by phone.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Stock › Reorder</strong> works out what to order per branch from reorder levels, the last 30 days' sales, lead
  times, what's already on order and minimum order sizes, grouped by supplier. <strong>Draft an order for …</strong> turns a supplier's
  suggestions into a draft order to check and send, and when several suppliers have suggestions, <strong>Draft … purchase orders</strong> does them all at once.
  <strong>Order them</strong> on Home takes you there.</p>
<h2>How the suggestions learn</h2>
<p>The reorder list learns from each branch's own history, so its suggestions follow what really happens:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What customers wanted, not just what sold.</strong> When the till turned someone away because the shelf was short, that
    counts too (once a day, however often the cashier tried).</li>
  <li><strong>The season.</strong> Once a branch has a year of sales, the weeks ahead are compared with the same weeks last year, so the
    rains or the building season show up before they arrive (at most twice, or half, the usual).</li>
  <li><strong>Busy days.</strong> A product whose sales jump about gets a margin on top, so a big day doesn't empty the shelf.</li>
  <li><strong>How long suppliers really take.</strong> Once a supplier has delivered two orders, the time from sending the order to the
    delivery arriving replaces the lead time typed in; the list says <em>measured</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each line says what changed its answer. Where the branch's sales point to a different reorder level, <strong>suggest … · Use</strong> sets
  it for that branch, and <strong>Use … suggested levels</strong> at the top sets them all (for whoever looks after the catalogue).</p>
<p>Use <strong>Group By › Supplier</strong> on the orders list to see what's still to come from each supplier.</p>

<%= render "help/walkthrough", tour: HelpTour.find("purchase-order") %>

<h2>Receiving a delivery</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Open the order and press <strong>Receive goods</strong>, or go to <strong>Buy › Receiving › New</strong> for goods that came
    without an order.</li>
  <li>Enter what arrived and what it cost, the supplier's delivery note number, and any transport, duty or other costs.</li>
  <li>Press <strong>Receive into stock</strong>. The goods go into the branch, the extra costs are spread over the lines by value, and each
    product's cost becomes the average of the old and new stock.</li>
</ol>
<p>Deliveries can also be checked in <%= link_to "on your phone", help_article_path("stock-on-your-phone"), class: "link" %>. Products
  tracked by batch ask for the batch number and expiry date.</p>
<p>If the supplier can't send the rest of a partly delivered order, open it and press <strong>Close short</strong>. The order shows what
  never came, and those goods stop counting as on order, so <strong>Stock › Reorder</strong> suggests them again.</p>

<%= render "help/walkthrough", tour: HelpTour.find("receiving") %>

<h2>Invoices and payments</h2>
<p>When the supplier's invoice comes, press <strong>Record the supplier's invoice</strong> on the delivery, or <strong>Record invoice</strong>
  on the supplier. An invoice for a delivery starts at the value of the goods, in the supplier's currency, without the transport or duty you paid
  yourself; if what you record differs from that by more than a little, HardPoint says so, and a delivery takes one invoice. <strong>Record payment</strong> settles their oldest invoices first. <strong>Buy › What we owe</strong> shows every
  supplier's balance by age: not yet due, 1–30, 31–60, 61–90 and over 90 days.</p>
<p>Something wrong with a delivery? See <%= link_to "Sending goods back to a supplier", help_article_path("supplier-returns"), class: "link" %>.</p>
